 here was built to use SPOOL/MNEWSinstead.  SRC/mnews/README.OSK lists that change.They do share one layer: both ride on UUCP to reach another site.  MNews'ssbatch hands its batches to `uux', and its rnews mails throughCMDS/UUCP/rmail, both from the UUCP set in CMDS/UUCP.UUCPbb's news -- the small set beside UUCPbb's mail---------------------------------------------------  Part of UUCPbb 2.1, Bob Billson's UUCP for OS-9, GPL v2.  rnews and  expire are from its patch 1.  The manuals are in DOC/uucpbb, already  formatted: readnews.man, postnews.man, rnews.man, subscribe.man.    rnews        takes a batch apart and files each article; a compressed                 batch it hands to `compress'    readnews     reads news a prompt at a time: `y', `n', `q', and `h' at                 any prompt for the commands.  New groups in the active                 file are offered one by one.  .newsrc in your home                 directory keeps your place    postnews     asks for the header fields, then posts the article                 locally and queues it for your UUCP neighbour.  The                 Distribution must be one listed in                 SYS/UUCP/distributions    expire       deletes articles past their date    subscribe, unsubscribe                 turn a group on or off in .newsrc  WHAT IT NEEDS: TERM set -- readnews stops with "Unknown terminal type"  without one -- and SPOOL/news in place before rnews or readnews will  start.  postnews forks `rnews' by name, so UUCP/rnews has to be  resident; without it, measured:      docmd_na(): cannot spawn process--> rnews -x0 -ncomp.os.os9 ...  With it loaded, the same posting lands as SPOOL/news/COMP/OS/OS9/a1.  Costs: no full-screen reader, and all six need cio.  `unsubscribe' on a  group that is already off prints a number where the group's name should  be -- a `%' in its message with no conversion letter after it.  To leave readnews: `q'.C News -- six tools from a larger system----------------------------------------  C News is Geoff Collyer and Henry Spencer's news transport, University  of Toronto, 1985-1990: everything in a news system except the reader.  blarson's OS-9 port arrived in an archive named GNU_ATP_1_40.lzh, which  is why that name turns up in its provenance.  Terms: use and  redistribution by anyone, with four conditions about credit --  DOC/cnews/COPYRIGHT.  The six tools here are C News's helpers:    bdecode, c7decode   undo the encodings a news batch is put into to                        cross a link that loses the eighth bit    dbz                 builds the history index that finds an article                        by its message-id    newshist            looks message-ids up in that history    byteflip            reorders the bytes of a dbz index made on another                        machine    newslock            the news system's lock, made by linking one name                        to another; this C library has no link(), so                        here it returns 1 and makes none  The program that files articles, relaynews, is C News's relay; its  source is SRC/cnews/relay, with the rest of the system beside it, and  DOC/cnews holds the README, the ROADMAP and eleven manual pages.  Take these if you run C News already, or want its tools to hand.  They  do not file, read or post an article.MNews and nn -- a news system and a full-screen reader------------------------------------------------------  Ulrich Dessauer's MNews, a news system written for OS-9 (1990-92), with  his port of Kim F. Storm's nn 6.3 newsreader.  All eleven are built here  from SRC/mnews, trap-free.  Dessauer's terms ask that the package travel  WHOLE, so SRC/mnews is the complete package; SOURCES.txt, under `MNEWS  AND NN', has the terms in full.    inews        files an article: `inews -h < article' posts one,                 `inews -c=newgroup:<group>' makes a group    rnews        unpacks a batch from another site and hands each article                 to inews    sbatch       collects articles for a neighbour into batches, and hands                 them to uux    nn           the reader: a full-screen menu of articles by subject,                 threads, kill files, `?' for help    nnmaster     keeps nn's database, SPOOL/NN    nnaux        what nn runs to post, follow up or reply    nncheck      says whether there is news you have not read    nnadmin      looks inside nn's database; `nnadmin C' shows where                 everything is    encode, decode, cvt_help                 B News 2.11's seven-bit coder, and nn's help converter  WHAT IT NEEDS, IN ORDER.  Each of these was measured 2026-09-23:  1. `setup', once each time the system starts, before anything else.     Every MNews program reads TOP Munich's SysInfo table -- host name, spool     directories, locks -- and setup builds it from SYS/sysinfo.  Without     it inews stops at once with `inews: Can't initialize the mess'.  2. Your own OS-9's `shell', for nnmaster.  nnmaster runs commands     through `shell'; on a real OS-9 system that is /dd/CMDS/shell and is     always there.  Under os9exec, SYS/login loads yours from /h1.     Without it `nnmaster -I' prints `Initializing master data base...'     and never returns.  With it, `nnmaster -I' (answer OK, then y to     reuse the GROUPS file) and then `nnmaster' build the database nn     reads.  3. inews resident before rnews runs.  rnews forks `inews' by bare name,     and inews lives in CMDS/MNEWS, not in the execution directory.     Without it rnews does not stop: it repeats         Can't execute 'inews'     for as long as you let it.  `load /dd/CMDS/MNEWS/inews' first and     the same batch reports `Received 1 articles'.  4. uux resident before sbatch runs, for the same reason -- otherwise     `Can't fork 'uux ...' (errno = 221)'.  5. For a compressed batch, a module named `uncompress': rnews forks it     by that name.  6. HOME and USER set, and TERM for nn.  nn keeps each reader's place in     .nn under HOME, and its first run makes that directory by running     `makdir' through `shell' -- your own OS-9's, both of them, which     SYS/login loads from /h1 under os9exec.  With neither resident nn     still opens its welcome screen, but makes no .nn, and nncheck goes     on saying `nn has not been invoked to initialize user files'.  Costs: the largest of the three by far, and several steps before the  first article can be read.  A period bug in inews writes the Date:  header's year as years since 1900 -- an article posted in 2026 says  `Date: 23 Sep 126'.  To leave nn: Q.CHOOSING--------  You want a full-screen newsreader                      MNews + nn  You have no cio and cannot get one                     MNews + nn  You run UUCPbb for mail and want news beside it        UUCPbb  You want the fewest steps before the first article     UUCPbb  You run C News already                                 C News's six  If you take exactly one: MNews with nn -- the whole of CMDS/MNEWS, with  `setup', SYS/sysinfo, USR/LIB/NEWS, USR/LIB/NN and the SPOOL  directories.  Run `setup' first, and load inews before rnews.